// Title: Application Termination Attempt via Wmic.EXE
// Sigma ID: 49d9671b-0a0a-4c09-8280-d215bfd30662
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Nasreddine Bencherchali (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2023-09-11 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: execution, T1047
// Description: Detects an attempt to terminate a process via "wmic" with the "call terminate" flag. Adversaries
//   may use wmic to terminate security products or other applications on the compromised host. This
//   event is triggered on on attempt and process creation can be either successful or unsuccessful.
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://cyble.com/blog/lockfile-ransomware-using-proxyshell-attack-to-deploy-ransomware/
//   - https://www.bitdefender.com/files/News/CaseStudies/study/377/Bitdefender-Whitepaper-WMI-creat4871-en-EN-GenericUse.pdf
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_wmic_terminate_application.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, microsoft_xdr schema)

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "\\WMIC.exe" or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName =~ "wmic.exe") and (ProcessCommandLine contains "call" and ProcessCommandLine contains "terminate")
